      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "iterative is the area-cheapest family (single shared datapath, no k-way parallelism) and will anchor the low-luts_plus_ffs end of the front; it will fail the throughput/batch constraints but its accuracy-vs-area trade-off is still needed for hypervolume coverage of the feasible region's boundary."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands 32 Msps throughput and a 0.44us p99 batch for 32 simultaneous requests, which only a 1-result/cycle architecture can meet; among my allowed families, unrolled_k is the only one that can do that, so I weight it 60% and sweep k=2..8 to find the smallest k that clears the batch bound while keeping area low. iterative is the area floor and I keep 40% of the budget there to map the accuracy-vs-area curve (it will be infeasible on throughput but contributes to the front's hypervolume). data_width 16-20 and n_iter 14-18 are chosen to sit just above the 2^-12 accuracy floor with rounding, and angle/frac guards 0-2 to explore the accuracy-cost trade-off without wasting bits."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The current best throughput is 10.5 MSPS, which is far below the 32 MSPS requirement. The `iterative` and `unrolled_k` families are limited by their latency (N+3 and ceil(N/k)+3 cycles respectively). To meet the 32 MSPS throughput constraint, we need a design with a high Fmax and a very low latency (ideally 1 result/cycle). The `pipelined` family provides 1 result/cycle throughput. If we can achieve a high enough Fmax (e.g., > 32 MHz) with a pipelined design, we will meet the throughput constraint. The `sys_p99_batch_us` constraint is also tight (0.44 us), which favors low latency. The `pipelined` family has a latency of N+2 cycles. With N=14-16, the latency is 16-18 cycles. If Fmax is ~50 MHz, latency is ~320-360 ns, which fits the 0.44 us budget. We must explore `pipelined` to see if it can meet the throughput and latency constraints.",
